SF CRIS                                                                         
OB To provide ecologically-based guidelines for the development of refor        
   estation and vegetation management prescriptions that enhance timber,        
    range, wildlife, and watershed values in harvested upper slope mixed        
    conifer forests of the interior Pacific Northwest.                          
AP Problem 1 - Land managers need additional information on responses of        
    out-planted conifer seedlings to environmental factors on newly harv        
   ested sites to develop more effective reforestation prescriptions. St        
   udies will characterize reforestation environments and determine how         
   outplanted seedlings of major conifer species respond to specific sit        
   e factors in terms of survival, growth, and physiological functioning        
   . Problem 2 - A knowledge of factors which influence site productivit        
   y and post-harvest successional trends and treatment alternatives is         
   needed to develop vegetation management prescriptions for achieving m        
   anagement objectives. Studies will describe vegetation development, n        
   utrient distribution and residue decomposition on postharvest sites;         
   specific vegetation management alternatives such as seeding and ungul        
   ate grazing will be evaluated.                                               
DE FORESTRY FOREST-MANAGEMENT MIXED-CONIFERS REFORESTATION ENVIRONMENTAL        
   -FACTORS SITE-PRODUCTIVITY FOREST-VEGETATION SEEDLING-GROWTH SEEDLING        
   -SURVIVAL FOREST-SUCCESSION MICROCLIMATE NUTRIENT-CYCLE GRAZING TIMBE        
   R-HARVESTING WILDLIFE RANGES FOREST-WATERSHEDS SEEDLINGS                     
PR As timber harvests progress into upper slope forests, managers encoun        
   ter site conditions that limit the establishment and growth of tree s        
   eedlings and other preferred vegetation. These conditions may be acce        
   ntuated by harvest methods and other silvicultural practices. Researc        
   h underway focuses on characterizing regeneration environments, provi        
   ding a predictive understanding of successional trends, and determini        
   ng the effects of selected factors on the physiological functioning,         
   establishment and growth of outplanted tree seedlings. Harvest-induce        
   d changes in the forest environment are being measured at several loc        
   ations in subalpine fir, lodgepole pine, and Pacific silver fir-hemlo        
   ck forests. The importance of this information was underscored by the        
    results of a laboratory study showing that low soil temperature redu        
   ced shoot growth and root elongation of Douglas-fir seedlings and is         
   likely an important factor contributing to poor survival of seedlings        
    outplanted at high elevation sites. Laboratory and field studies wit        
   h other important upper slope conifers are planned. University cooper        
   ators are completing related studies that will provide information on        
    nutrient capital distribution and residue decomposition processes in        
    upper slope forests. Information on vegetation dynamics of harvested        
    sites is needed to develop vegetation management alternatives.              
PB LOPUSHINSKY, W. and KAUFMANN, M.R. 1984. Effects of cold soil on wate        
